        <div id="how-much-wood" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Start With How Much Wood You Split
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most useful number in choosing a splitter is not tons, horsepower or price; it is how many cords you split a year. A cord is a stack 4 feet high, 4 feet deep and 8 feet long, or 128 cubic feet, and it is the standard measure for firewood in the US. Someone who burns a face cord or two in a backyard fire pit needs a completely different machine from someone heating a house with six to eight full cords every winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Under a cord a year, a small electric or manual splitter, or even a good maul, is enough. From one to three cords, a quality electric splitter can work if your wood is seasoned and of moderate size, but a mid-size gas splitter saves real time. Above three cords, especially with big hardwood rounds, a 20 to 27-ton gas splitter with vertical operation is the right tool, and owners at the high end often value speed and auto-return features above everything else.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Annual volume</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sensible machine</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pick to consider</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under one cord</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Manual or small electric</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sun Joe LJ10M, WEN 56207</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">One to two cords</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electric 7 ton or small gas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Boss ES7T20, Powerhorse 22-Ton</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two to five cords</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gas 22 to 27 ton, vertical</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Champion 27-Ton, YARDMAX YU2566</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Five cords and up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Robust 25 to 27-ton gas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cub Cadet LS 27, Champion 27-Ton</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Consider the wood itself too. Straight-grained, seasoned oak, ash and maple split far more easily than green elm, sweetgum or knotty crotch wood. If you cut your own trees with a <a href="/best-chainsaw/">chainsaw</a>, you will get a mix, including tough pieces from near the base and branches; buy for the hard pieces, not the easy ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, factor in your body. Lifting 60 to 100-pound rounds onto a waist-high beam all day is hard work. If you split big rounds, vertical operation is not a luxury; it lets you roll the round onto the foot plate instead of lifting it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Space and storage belong in the calculation as well. A towable gas splitter takes up roughly the footprint of a small trailer and needs a level spot to work and a dry place to sit the rest of the year. An electric or manual unit fits on a garage shelf or in a corner. If you have a small lot and a tight garage, that alone may settle the question in favor of electric, provided your wood is seasoned and of moderate size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tonnage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Tonnage Really Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            Splitter tonnage is the maximum force the hydraulic ram can exert, calculated from the cylinder size and the pump's maximum pressure. It tells you whether the machine can push through a tough round, but beyond a certain point more tons do not split typical firewood any faster. Ratings also vary in how they are calculated and advertised, so a 25-ton machine from one brand is not always weaker than a 27-ton from another.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a rough guide, small electric splitters in the 4 to 7-ton range handle seasoned, straight-grained rounds of modest diameter. Gas splitters in the 20 to 27-ton range handle nearly all homeowner firewood, including larger and knottier rounds. Commercial machines above that are for production firewood and very large, stringy wood.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Tonnage at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4-7 T</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Electric, seasoned moderate rounds</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">10 T</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Manual hydraulic, slow but steady</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-27 T</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Most homeowner gas splitters</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">128 ft³</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Volume of one full cord</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Wood condition changes the math. Seasoned wood with checking cracks at the ends splits much more easily than freshly cut green wood. Some species, especially elm and sweetgum, have interlocked grain that resists splitting no matter the tonnage, and they are slow even on big machines. Knots and crotches need far more force than straight sections.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy enough tonnage for the toughest wood you split regularly, then spend the rest of your budget on speed, vertical operation and build quality. A 22-ton machine with a fast cycle will usually get through a pile faster than a 30-ton machine with a slow one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cycle-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Cycle Time: The Spec That Decides Your Weekend
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cycle time is how long the ram takes to extend fully and return. Many gas splitters list cycle times somewhere in the 10 to 20-second range, while small electric splitters are typically slower. Over hundreds of splits, the difference adds up to hours. On a big pile, a faster cycle means less waiting with your hands idle, which also means less temptation to reach in while the ram is moving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two-stage pumps are the reason gas splitters cycle fast. They move the ram quickly at low pressure until it meets resistance, then switch to high-pressure, slow movement to split the wood. When the log pops, the pump shifts back to high speed. Look for a two-stage pump on any gas splitter you consider.
          </p>

          <p>
            An auto-return valve also saves time. After the split, you move the lever to the return position and let go; a detent holds it there and drops back to neutral when the ram is fully home, freeing your hands to set up the next piece. Without it, you hold the lever through the whole return stroke.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SPEED VS SAFETY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A faster cycle is not a reason to rush.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Never position or adjust a log while the ram is moving, even on a slow machine. Wait for the ram to stop or retract fully, then reposition. Speed should come from the machine, not from shortcuts with your hands.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Do not compare cycle times across brands too literally, since test conditions vary. Use them to separate fast machines from slow ones within the same class, and weigh them alongside tonnage and features.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Gas, Electric, Manual and Kinetic Splitters
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Gas hydraulic</strong> splitters are the standard for serious firewood. They use a small gas engine to drive a hydraulic pump, offer 20 tons or more, often convert to vertical operation, and tow behind a vehicle. They are loud, need engine maintenance and are too big for many small properties, but they handle volume and difficult wood.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Electric hydraulic</strong> splitters run on household current and are quiet, clean and low-maintenance, so you can use them close to the house or in an open garage. They are typically 4 to 10 tons, horizontal-only, and slower than gas. They suit fireplace and fire pit volumes of seasoned wood. Use the extension cord gauge and length the manual specifies, or plug into a GFCI outlet directly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Manual hydraulic</strong> splitters use a hand-pumped jack to push the ram. They need no power, are quiet and inexpensive, but each split takes many strokes, so they are practical only for small amounts. <strong>Kinetic</strong> splitters use a heavy spinning flywheel to drive the ram very quickly; they are fast but generally more expensive and require extra caution because the ram moves fast.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
