        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Tonnage Instead of Speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most homeowner firewood splits within the range of a 20 to 27-ton gas machine, and extra tons beyond that rarely make the job faster. Cycle time, a two-stage pump and auto-return decide how long your pile takes. Buy enough force for your toughest regular wood, then prioritize speed and features.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Two-Person Operation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Having one person load while another works the control is a classic way to crush fingers, because the operator cannot see or feel exactly where the loader's hands are. Manufacturers warn against it. One person should position the log and run the control, with helpers well clear of the machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Lifting Heavy Rounds Onto the Beam</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lifting big rounds to waist height again and again strains backs and leads to dropped logs on feet. If you split large rounds, buy a splitter with vertical operation and roll heavy pieces onto the foot plate instead. Use a hookaroon to move rounds without bending.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Gas Splitter in a Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gas engines produce carbon monoxide, which can build up to dangerous levels quickly in enclosed spaces, even with a door open. Always run gas splitters outdoors, away from doors and windows. If you need to split close to the house, choose an electric model. Carbon monoxide is invisible and odorless.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Feeling for Hydraulic Leaks by Hand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pinhole leak in a pressurized line can inject hydraulic fluid through skin, a serious injury requiring emergency treatment. Check suspected leaks with cardboard, relieve pressure before working on fittings, and replace damaged hoses rather than patching them. Any injection injury, even one that looks minor, needs emergency medical care right away.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many tons of splitting force do I actually need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For seasoned, straight-grained firewood of moderate diameter, a 4 to 7-ton electric splitter is enough. For most homeowner firewood, including larger and knottier rounds, a 20 to 27-ton gas splitter is the practical range. Very large, green or stringy wood such as elm benefits from the higher end. Beyond that, speed and features matter more than extra tons.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an electric splitter good enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For fireplace and fire pit volumes of seasoned wood, yes. Electric splitters are quiet, clean and low-maintenance, and they can be used near the house. They are slower than gas, usually horizontal only, and can stall on large, knotty or green wood. If you split more than a cord or two a year, or your wood is big, a gas splitter is a better fit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I rent a splitter instead of buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Renting makes sense if you split once a year and handle a cord or two. Have the wood cut and staged before you pick up the machine to make the most of the rental period. If you split every year or several cords at a time, buying usually pays for itself within a few seasons, especially if you share a machine with neighbors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What cycle time should I look for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many gas splitters list cycle times somewhere in the 10 to 20-second range; faster is better for big piles. Small electric splitters are generally slower. A two-stage pump and auto-return valve improve speed noticeably. Use cycle times to compare machines within a class, since test conditions vary between brands. Auto-return helps too.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is vertical splitting worth paying extra for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you split large, heavy rounds, yes. Vertical operation lets you roll a round onto the foot plate instead of lifting it onto a waist-high beam, which saves your back and makes very heavy pieces manageable. If you only split moderate pieces that are easy to lift, horizontal-only is fine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should firewood season after splitting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most hardwoods need at least six months and often a year or more to dry, stacked off the ground in a sunny, breezy spot with the top covered and the sides open. The EPA's Burn Wise program recommends a moisture content of 20 percent or less for clean burning, and a moisture meter is an inexpensive way to check.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can two people run a log splitter together?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers warn against it. When one person loads and another runs the control, the operator cannot reliably see where the loader's hands are, and crushing injuries result. The safe practice is one operator who both positions the log and runs the control, with any helpers stacking splits well clear of the machine.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

